    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A Corsican artillery officer who rose through the wars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, Napoleon seized power in the coup of November 1799 and crowned himself Emperor of the French in December 1804.</li><li>His victories from Austerlitz to Wagram brought about 40% of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s population under his direct or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indirect-rule">indirect rule</a>, while his civil code, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prefect">prefects</a>, lycées and Concordat reshaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>; he also ended the free press and reinstated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in French colonies.</li><li>The war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1812 broke his power: he abdicated in 1814, returned for the Hundred Days, was defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a> in June 1815 and died a British prisoner on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-helena">Saint Helena</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> the British press mocked Napoleon as a short-tempered small man, nicknamed &quot;Little Boney&quot;. In fact, at about 168 cm (5 ft 6 in), he was of average height.</p></aside>
    </section>

