    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1769</span><span class="rf-text">Napoleon is born in Ajaccio, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a>, one year after the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> ceded the island to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1793</span><span class="rf-text">As artillery commander he plans the assault that recaptures the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulon">Toulon</a> and is promoted to brigadier general</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1799</span><span class="rf-text">After campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> he overthrows the Directory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coup-of-18-brumaire">Coup of 18 Brumaire</a> and becomes First Consul</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1804</span><span class="rf-text">His civil code is implemented in March; the senate proclaims him Emperor of the French in May and he crowns himself at Notre Dame in December</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1805</span><span class="rf-text">He defeats the Austrian and Russian armies at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-austerlitz">Battle of Austerlitz</a>, weeks after the British naval victory at Trafalgar</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1812</span><span class="rf-text">He invades <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> with around 450,000 frontline troops, occupies <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and loses most of his army in the retreat</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1815</span><span class="rf-text">Escaping from exile on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elba">Elba</a>, he retakes power for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days</a>, is defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a> and is sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-helena">Saint Helena</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1821</span><span class="rf-text">Napoleon dies on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-helena">Saint Helena</a> at the age of 51</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Napoleon was born on 15 August 1769 in the Maison Bonaparte in Ajaccio, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a>, and baptized Napoleone di Buonaparte. His family was of Italian origin: his father's ancestors descended from a minor Tuscan noble family that emigrated to Corsica in the 16th century, his mother's from a noble family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>. He was the second of the eight surviving children of Carlo Maria Buonaparte and Maria Letizia Ramolino. He was born one year after the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> ceded Corsica to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. His father had supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasquale-paoli">Pasquale Paoli</a> in the Corsican war of independence, but after the Corsican defeat in 1769 he became friends with the French governor, Charles Louis de Marbeuf, with whose support Napoleon obtained a royal bursary to a military academy in mainland France.</p><p>In January 1779, aged 9, he moved to the mainland, first to a religious school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autun">Autun</a> to improve his French, his mother tongues being Corsican and Italian, and in May to the military academy at Brienne-le-Château. There he was routinely bullied for his accent, birthplace, short stature and poor French; he became reserved and melancholic and applied himself to reading.</p><p>In September 1784 he was admitted to the École militaire in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> to train as an artillery officer. His father's death in February 1785 cut the family income and forced him to complete the two-year course in one. In September 1785 he became the first Corsican to graduate from the school and was commissioned a second lieutenant in La Fère artillery regiment.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Bonaparte supported the Revolution of 1789 and promoted its cause in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a>, where he joined the pro-French republicans against Paoli; in 1793 the Buonapartes fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulon">Toulon</a>. That September he was appointed artillery commander of the republican forces sent to recapture the port, and his plan to take a hill fort dominating the harbour led to the city's capture in December. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilien-robespierre">Maximilien Robespierre</a> in July 1794 his Jacobin connections made him suspect, and he was arrested for two weeks.</p><p>On 5 October 1795, 13 Vendémiaire, he defended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a> against a royalist rebellion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, firing on the rebels with canister rounds; about 300 to 1,400 rebels died. The new government, the Directory, made him head of the Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in January 1796. On 9 March he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephine-de-beauharnais">Joséphine de Beauharnais</a>. In Italy he knocked the Piedmontese out of the war in two weeks and defeated every Austrian effort to relieve Mantua; the victory at Rivoli in January 1797 led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-campo-formio">Treaty of Campo Formio</a>, and he returned to Paris a hero.</p><p>In 1798 he led an expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-pyramids">Battle of the Pyramids</a> on 21 July, but on 1 August the British fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Horatio Nelson</a> destroyed the French fleet. He left his army in August 1799 without explicit orders. On 9 November 1799, 18 Brumaire, he and his allies overthrew the Directory, and the Constitution of the Year VIII gave real power to Bonaparte as First Consul. The plebiscite that approved it officially counted over three million in favour, his brother Lucien having doubled the "yes" vote.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The victory at Marengo on 14 June 1800 increased Bonaparte's authority, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-amiens">Treaty of Amiens</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in March 1802 brought a decade of war to an end. By the Concordat of 1801 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-vii">Pope Pius VII</a> recognized his regime and the regime recognized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> as the majority religion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. A plebiscite in 1802 made him consul for life. He established <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prefect">prefects</a> to run the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>, the Council of State, the Banque de France, secondary schools and lycées, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> of Honour. His civil code, implemented in March 1804 and known from 1807 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-code">Napoleonic Code</a>, entrenched equality before the law, religious toleration, secure property rights and the abolition of the vestiges of feudalism.</p><p>On 18 May 1804 the senate proclaimed him Emperor of the French, and on 2 December, at Notre Dame de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, he crowned himself after being anointed by the pope. In 1805 he captured 60,000 Austrian soldiers at Ulm and on 2 December defeated the Austrians and Russians at Austerlitz, where about a third of the allied forces were killed, captured or wounded. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a>, established in 1806, spelled the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><p>He defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> and Auerstedt on 14 October 1806 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> at Friedland on 14 June 1807; by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-tilsit">Treaties of Tilsit</a> Prussia lost half its territory and population. He placed his brothers on the thrones of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, Holland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-wagram">Battle of Wagram</a> in July 1809 he ruled about 40% of the European population, directly or through satellite kingdoms.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>A succession of coalitions of European powers fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> declared war again in May 1803; after Trafalgar his fleet never again seriously challenged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>. His installation of his brother Joseph as King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1808 provoked an uprising met with brutal French repression and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war in which both sides committed atrocities; Napoleon later called it "the unlucky war [that] ruined me". <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-vii">Pope Pius VII</a> excommunicated him in 1809 and was arrested and held until 1814.</p><p>At home he introduced censorship, closed most opposition newspapers and ended directly elected representative government. In 1804 he had the Duke of Enghien kidnapped and executed after a secret trial, though there was no proof of his involvement in a royalist plot. In May 1802 he reintroduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in the recovered colonies, and the expedition he sent to retake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> failed; the former slaves proclaimed the republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> in 1804. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a> in 1799 he ordered the garrison and some 1,500–5,000 prisoners to be executed.</p><p>Critics cite his exploitation of conquered territories and the plunder of art for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>. Historians have argued that his expansionist foreign policy was a major factor in the Napoleonic wars, which cost six million lives. Frank McLynn and Correlli Barnett suggest that his reputation as a military genius is exaggerated. Pieter Geyl in 1947 and Claude Ribbe in 2005 compared him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a>; most modern critics reject the comparison, arguing that he did not commit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> or the mass murder of his political opponents.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On 24 June 1812 Napoleon's multinational <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grande-armee">Grande Armée</a>, around 450,000 frontline troops, began crossing into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. After the Battle of Borodino on 7 September, which left 44,000 Russian and 35,000 French dead, wounded or captured, he entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> Alexander refused to negotiate and after six weeks the army withdrew through cold, disease and hunger. About 75,000 troops recrossed the Nieman; total military deaths on both sides were up to one million. In 1813 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> joined Russia, and the coalition won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leipzig">Battle of Leipzig</a> in October. The allies entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> capitulated on 31 March 1814, and Napoleon abdicated unconditionally on 6 April.</p><p>Exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elba">Elba</a>, he escaped on 26 February 1815 with about 1,000 men, was joined by the troops sent to stop him and entered Paris on 20 March. The powers declared him an outlaw. On 18 June Wellington's army withstood his attacks at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher">Blücher</a>'s Prussians arrived on his right flank. He abdicated on 22 June in favour of his son, whom the allies refused to recognize, and on 15 July surrendered to the British on HMS <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bellerophon">Bellerophon</a>.</p><p>He was held on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-helena">Saint Helena</a> in the Atlantic, at Longwood House, a damp and unhealthy bungalow, guarded by 2,100 soldiers. His health worsened from mid-1817, and he died on 5 May 1821 at age 51. Two autopsy reports concluded that he died of internal bleeding caused by stomach cancer; a later theory of arsenic poisoning was undermined by further studies. In 1840 his remains were returned to France, and in 1861 they were entombed at Les Invalides.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Napoleon's reforms outlasted him. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-code">Napoleonic Code</a>, carried across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> by his armies, continued to influence the law in western Europe, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>; Alfred Cobban called it "the most effective agency for the propagation of the basic principles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>". His regime abolished remnants of feudalism in conquered lands, closed the Jewish ghettos and ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-inquisition">Spanish Inquisition</a>. His schools laid the foundation of modern secondary and tertiary education in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p>His reorganization of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and his rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> sparked nationalism and the movements toward German and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Italian unification</a>, and the ousting of the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a> indirectly began the process of Latin American independence. His reputation is generally favourable in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, the only country to evoke him in its national anthem.</p><p>A legend grew from his own propaganda and from The Memorial of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-helena">Saint Helena</a>, published by Las Cases in 1822, which presented him as a liberal deposed by reactionaries. It fed defiance of the restored Bourbons, and David A. Bell argues that the regime of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> was possible only because of it. Historians remain divided on whether he was "an enlightened despot who laid the foundations of modern Europe" or "a megalomaniac who wrought greater misery than any man before the coming of Hitler". Carl von Clausewitz regarded him as a genius in the art of war. Pieter Geyl wrote in 1947 that it is impossible for two historians, especially of different periods, to see any historical personality in the same light.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Napoleon Bonaparte rose from Corsican artillery officer to First Consul of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1799 and Emperor of the French in 1804. He defeated the armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and gave France its civil code, but the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the invasion of Russia in 1812 led to his abdication in 1814. Defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a> in 1815, he died in exile on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-helena">Saint Helena</a> in 1821.</em></p>

